## Performance of physical $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage technologies
As described in the Scope of the Analysis Section, the conventional compressed hydrogen (Comp- $\begin{array}{r}{.\mathrm{H}_{2,}^{\cdot\mathrm{~}}}\end{array}$ ) storage under 170 and 350 bar, and liquid hydrogen (Liq- $\begin{array}{r}{.\mathsf{H}_{2}}\end{array}$ ) storage under $19\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar are used as the reference cases to compare with MOF storage. Salt cavern storage, and numerous other porous geologic formations presently used to store natural gas, are viable physical alternatives for storing large volumes of hydrogen. However, we limit this analysis to above-ground storage solutions that can be integrated into facilities without geographical limitations, thus these geological methods are not considered. From Fig. 1a, Fig. 1c and Fig. 2a, we find that when the MOFs are packed loosely (under bed and pellet porosities of 0.6), all of the modeled MOFs are more economical than Liq- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage, and have the potential of being economically comparable with Comp- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage. Furthermore, Liq- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is not suitable for large-scale backup power supplies as it requires extremely low cryogenic temperature (below 20 K)35. As a result, significant boil-off control and handling is required, regardless of how frequently the backup power system is used36.
